  The Fedora copyright line is kept because this file is Fedora's, copied
  rather than rewritten: their descriptions are GPL-3.0-or-later, and a
  derived file keeps the notice. OpenQOS's contribution is the delta
  described below and nothing else.

  The shared desktop baseline, and the one place OpenQOS deviates from Fedora's
  desktop profile on purpose.

  This file replaces upstream/components/desktops/common.xml at the pinned
  revision dfc49a5a10f69941179fdadd96aa6a5984f7c677. It is not an addition to it:
  kiwi/OpenQOS.kiwi includes this file *instead* of upstream's, because the only
  thing that differs is one line and KIWI has no way to remove a package from
  another section. `<ignore>` cannot do it — it becomes dnf's exclude, and dnf
  refuses a transaction that both requests and excludes the same package:

      argument 'mediawriter' matches only excluded packages

  which is exactly what upstream's line does, since it names the package
  explicitly.

  The delta from upstream is:

    - <package name="mediawriter"/>   removed
    + <ignore  name="mediawriter"/>   added in its place

  Why remove it: Fedora Media Writer is Fedora's own image-writing utility. It is
  not a generic tool, its interface is Fedora's, and its purpose on a desktop is to
  write Fedora images. Shipping it on OpenQOS means shipping a Fedora-branded
  application that does not write OpenQOS media, and nothing in the image requires
  it — no package depends on it, so removing it costs nothing.

  What is *not* possible yet is replacing it. That needs an OpenQOS image writer,
  which is not a branding change but a piece of software; it is recorded as open
  work rather than pretended away with a package that provides a name it does not
  implement.

  Everything else here is upstream's, copied deliberately rather than
  reconstructed, so that a reader can diff the two files and see only the delta.
  The cost of owning this file is that future additions upstream makes to the
  desktop baseline will not reach us automatically; tests/check-upstream-drift.sh
  exists to make that visible rather than silent.
-->
<image>
	<profiles>
		<profile name="DesktopCommon" description="Common desktop packages">
			<requires profile="BaseCommon"/>
			<requires profile="HardwareCommon"/>
		</profile>
	</profiles>
	<packages type="image" patternType="plusRecommended" profiles="DesktopCommon">
		<namedCollection name="fonts"/>
		<namedCollection name="multimedia"/>
		<namedCollection name="networkmanager-submodules"/>
		<namedCollection name="printing"/>
		<namedCollection name="guest-desktop-agents"/>
		<ignore name="mediawriter"/>
		<ignore name="gfs2-utils"/>
		<ignore name="reiserfs-utils"/>
	</packages>
</image>
